artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a planet of mass $M_p$, radius $R_p$, and orbital semi-major axis $a$ around a host star of mass $M_\star$ that produces stellar flares. A flare event is characterized by a bolometric energy $E_f$, a time-resolved spectral energy distribution $F_\lambda(t)$ spanning X-ray, EUV, UV, and optical wavelengths, and an associated solar/stellar energetic particle (SEP) flux $\Phi_p(E,t)$ with energy spectrum in $E$ (protons and heavier ions). Assume the planet possesses an atmosphere with surface pressure $P_0$, composition vector $\{X_i\}$ (mole fractions of species $i$), and (optionally) a global magnetic dipole moment $\mathcal{M}$.

Develop a quantitative theoretical and computational framework that, given the flare statistics (occurrence frequency-energy distribution $dN/dE_f \propto E_f^{-\alpha}$ over the stellar lifetime), the spectral and particle properties of individual flare events, and the planetary parameters above, determines:

(a) the rate of atmospheric mass loss due to thermal (XUV-driven hydrodynamic) and non-thermal (ion pickup, sputtering, photochemical) escape processes induced by repeated flaring, and the conditions under which the atmosphere is retained over geological timescales;

(b) the steady-state and transient atmospheric chemical composition, including depletion of ozone or analogous UV-shielding species, production of $\mathrm{NO}_x$, $\mathrm{HO}_x$, and other photochemical and SEP-induced byproducts, as a function of the flare energy distribution and SED hardness;

(c) the surface ultraviolet and ionizing radiation dose received by the planet during and between flares, accounting for atmospheric attenuation and (where relevant) magnetospheric shielding from SEPs, and the consequent implications for surface conditions and biosignature stability.

Characterize the parameter regimes (in $E_f$, $\alpha$, $a$, $P_0$, $\{X_i\}$, $\mathcal{M}$) that delineate habitable from non-habitable outcomes under sustained flare forcing.
